piratecash-seeder
=================

Authoritative DNS seed backed by PirateCash Core JSON-RPC. The service does
not crawl, probe or connect to P2P nodes. Its only outgoing requests are to
the configured RPC endpoint.

How it works
------------

* At startup, calls `masternode list json` before accepting DNS queries.
* Polls RPC periodically (every 300 seconds by default), independently of DNS
  traffic. RPC requests have a configurable timeout.
* Publishes only masternodes whose status is exactly `ENABLED`, excluding
  `POSE_BANNED` and all other statuses. Uses `addresses.core_p2p`, with the
  legacy `address` field as a fallback for older Core versions. Platform HTTPS
  and platform P2P addresses are not used.
* Caches a deduplicated list of literal IPv4/IPv6 addresses in memory. Each DNS
  reply samples eligible addresses randomly without duplicates, as in dnseed.
  A/AAAA records contain only addresses of the requested family; packet size
  limits determine how many of the sampled records fit in a reply.
* On RPC connection failures, timeouts, HTTP errors, RPC errors or malformed
  responses, keeps serving the current cache until polling succeeds again.
  If RPC has not provided a usable list yet, serves the configured bootstrap
  addresses instead.
* A successful response with no active IP addresses replaces the old cache
  with bootstrap addresses. This avoids retaining nodes that RPC now reports
  as banned. If bootstrap is empty too, DNS returns no A/AAAA records.
* The cache is not persisted across restarts. Old crawler database files are
  neither read nor written.

Build and test
--------------

Requires a C++17 compiler, make and libcurl development headers/libraries.
UniValue (the Core JSON parser) is vendored under `vendor/univalue` with its
license and source revision; Boost and OpenSSL development headers are no
longer needed by the seeder itself.

Debian/Ubuntu:

    sudo apt-get install build-essential pkg-config libcurl4-openssl-dev python3
    make
    make test

macOS with MacPorts:

    sudo port install curl pkgconfig
    make
    make test

Tests use Python's standard library, a local mock RPC server and real UDP DNS
queries. They cover RPC authentication, filtering, address families, random
answers, concurrent queries, startup fallback, cache retention during errors
and timeouts, recovery and removal of newly banned nodes. Tests need permission
to open loopback TCP/UDP sockets.

Configuration
-------------

    cp .env.example .env
    chmod 600 .env

Edit `.env` in the working directory. It is ignored by Git. Environment
variables override `.env`; optional DNS command-line flags override both.
Values may be unquoted or enclosed in single/double quotes. Blank lines,
comments and an optional `export ` prefix are supported; shell interpolation
and backslash escape processing are not performed.

    RPC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:19606/
    RPC_USER=user
    RPC_PASSWORD=your-rpc-password
    RPC_POLL_INTERVAL=300
    RPC_TIMEOUT=10
    BOOTSTRAP_NODES=192.0.2.10,192.0.2.11,[2001:db8::10]:63636
    DNS_HOST=dnsseed.example.com
    DNS_NS=ns.example.com
    DNS_MBOX=hostmaster.example.com
    DNS_PORT=5353
    DNS_THREADS=4
    DNS_TTL=300
    DNS_NS_TTL=40000

Replace the documentation IP addresses above with known bootstrap nodes and
configure the real DNS host and nameserver. Bootstrap accepts comma/space-
separated numeric IPv4 or IPv6 addresses, optionally with a port; IPv6 services
with a port must use brackets. Ports are stripped for DNS answers. Hostnames
are deliberately rejected, so bootstrap loading never triggers peer discovery.
Unspecified, loopback and multicast addresses are rejected.

`RPC_URL` defaults to `http://127.0.0.1:19606/`; change it for a different
network or RPC endpoint. HTTP Basic authentication uses `RPC_USER` and
`RPC_PASSWORD`. HTTPS uses normal certificate verification. Requests go
directly to the configured endpoint without ambient HTTP proxy settings or
redirect following.

`RPC_POLL_INTERVAL` is the delay between the completion of a poll and the next
poll (1..86400 seconds). `RPC_TIMEOUT` limits each request (1..300 seconds).
`DNS_HOST` and `DNS_NS` are required. `DNS_MBOX` defaults to
`hostmaster.<DNS_HOST>`; use DNS mailbox notation with `@` replaced by `.`.
`DNS_PORT` defaults to 53, `DNS_THREADS` to 4, `DNS_TTL` to 300 and
`DNS_NS_TTL` to 40000. Use the example's unprivileged port 5353 for local tests.

Run
---

    ./dnsseed

Optional DNS overrides remain available:

    ./dnsseed -h dnsseed.example.com -n ns.example.com -m hostmaster.example.com -p 5353 -d 4
    dig @127.0.0.1 -p 5353 dnsseed.example.com A
    dig @127.0.0.1 -p 5353 dnsseed.example.com AAAA

For public operation, delegate the seed's zone to your nameserver with an NS
record in the parent zone and make the configured UDP port reachable. Port 53
normally requires elevated privileges or a platform-specific port redirect.

Crawler, proxy, ban database, service-flag filter and testnet discovery options
have been removed. RPC's masternode list does not provide P2P service bits, so
`x<flags>.<seed>` queries have no address records. Select the desired network
by setting its RPC endpoint and bootstrap list in `.env`.
